.Dd November 14, 2026
.Dt CPIO 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm cpio
.Nd formats of cpio archives
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A cpio archive is a sequence of members, each a header, the member's
name, and its data, ended by a member named
.Ql TRAILER\&!\&!\&! .
The data of a regular file is its contents and of a symbolic link its
target; other files have none.
.Xr cpio 1
reads and writes the formats below, and the two tar formats at the
end.
.Ss newc and crc
The header is 110 bytes of ASCII: a 6-byte magic and thirteen 8-digit
hexadecimal fields.
.Bl -column "c_filesize" "Bytes" -offset indent
.It Sy Field Ta Sy Bytes Ta Sy Contents
.It c_magic Ta 6 Ta Qq 070701 , or Qq 070702 for crc
.It c_ino Ta 8 Ta inode number
.It c_mode Ta 8 Ta file type and permissions
.It c_uid Ta 8 Ta owner
.It c_gid Ta 8 Ta group
.It c_nlink Ta 8 Ta link count
.It c_mtime Ta 8 Ta modification time, seconds since the Epoch
.It c_filesize Ta 8 Ta size of the data
.It c_devmajor Ta 8 Ta major number of the device holding the file
.It c_devminor Ta 8 Ta its minor number
.It c_rdevmajor Ta 8 Ta for a device file, its major number
.It c_rdevminor Ta 8 Ta and its minor number
.It c_namesize Ta 8 Ta length of the name, its NUL included
.It c_check Ta 8 Ta crc: the sum of the data's bytes, modulo 2^32; newc: 0
.El
.Pp
The name follows, with its NUL, padded with NULs to a multiple of 4
bytes counted from the start of the header; then the data, padded to a
multiple of 4.
.Pp
A file with several links is written once for each name, every name
but the last with a size of zero and the last with the data; a reader
links the names that share
.Va c_devmajor ,
.Va c_devminor
and
.Va c_ino .
.Ss odc
The header is 76 bytes of ASCII: a 6-byte magic and octal fields.
.Bl -column "c_filesize" "Bytes" -offset indent
.It Sy Field Ta Sy Bytes Ta Sy Contents
.It c_magic Ta 6 Ta Qq 070707
.It c_dev Ta 6 Ta device holding the file
.It c_ino Ta 6 Ta inode number
.It c_mode Ta 6 Ta file type and permissions
.It c_uid Ta 6 Ta owner
.It c_gid Ta 6 Ta group
.It c_nlink Ta 6 Ta link count
.It c_rdev Ta 6 Ta for a device file, its number
.It c_mtime Ta 11 Ta modification time
.It c_namesize Ta 6 Ta length of the name, its NUL included
.It c_filesize Ta 11 Ta size of the data
.El
.Pp
The name and the data follow unpadded.
Every name of a linked file carries the data.
This is the cpio interchange format of
.Xr pax 1 .
.Ss bin
The header is thirteen 16-bit words in the byte order of the machine
that wrote it: c_magic (070707 octal), c_dev, c_ino, c_mode, c_uid,
c_gid, c_nlink, c_rdev, c_mtime (two words, the high one first),
c_namesize and c_filesize (two words, the high one first).
The name and the data each follow padded to an even length.
A reader tells the byte order from the magic.
Every name of a linked file carries the data.
.Ss hpodc and hpbin
odc and bin as HP-UX writes them: for a device file
.Va c_rdev
holds 0,1 and
.Va c_filesize
the device's number.
The magic is odc's or bin's, so they cannot be told apart from those.
.Ss ustar and tar
A tar archive is a sequence of 512-byte blocks: for each file a header
block, then its data padded to a whole block; two blocks of zeros end
it.
Numeric fields are octal, ended by a NUL or a space.
.Bl -column "typeflag" "Offset" "Bytes" -offset indent
.It Sy Field Ta Sy Offset Ta Sy Bytes Ta Sy Contents
.It name Ta 0 Ta 100 Ta name; a directory's ends in Ql /
.It mode Ta 100 Ta 8 Ta permissions
.It uid Ta 108 Ta 8 Ta owner
.It gid Ta 116 Ta 8 Ta group
.It size Ta 124 Ta 12 Ta size of the data (regular files only)
.It mtime Ta 136 Ta 12 Ta modification time
.It chksum Ta 148 Ta 8 Ta sum of the header's bytes, this field as spaces
.It typeflag Ta 156 Ta 1 Ta the type: 0 file, 1 hard link, 2 symbolic link, 3 character device, 4 block device, 5 directory, 6 fifo
.It linkname Ta 157 Ta 100 Ta target of a link
.It magic Ta 257 Ta 6 Ta ustar: Qq ustar
.It version Ta 263 Ta 2 Ta ustar: Qq 00
.It uname Ta 265 Ta 32 Ta ustar: owner's name
.It gname Ta 297 Ta 32 Ta ustar: group's name
.It devmajor Ta 329 Ta 8 Ta ustar: a device's major number
.It devminor Ta 337 Ta 8 Ta ustar: its minor number
.It prefix Ta 345 Ta 155 Ta ustar: the start of a name longer than 100 bytes
.El
.Pp
The old tar format has only the fields up to
.Va linkname ,
types
.Ql 0
to
.Ql 2
and
.Ql 5 ,
and names of at most 100 bytes.
A file's second and later names are written as hard links to its first.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cpio 1 ,
.Xr pax 1 ,
.Xr tar 1
.Sh STANDARDS
The odc format is the cpio interchange format, and ustar the ustar
interchange format, of
.Xr pax 1
in
.St -p1003.1-2008
and later.
